{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-22-616-fall-2003", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["22.616"], "cross_listed_codes": [], "course_title": "Plasma Transport Theory", "course_slug": "22-616-plasma-transport-theory-fall-2003", "term": "Fall", "year": 2003, "as_taught_in": "Fall 2003", "language": "en", "departments": ["Nuclear Science and Engineering"], "academic_levels": ["Graduate"], "instructors": ["Prof. Kim Molvig"], "subject_primary": "Chemical Engineering", "subject_secondary": ["Transport Processes", "Science", "Chemistry", "Physical Chemistry", "Physics", "High Energy Physics", "Nuclear Physics"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Engineering", "subtopics": [{"name": "Chemical Engineering", "specialties": ["Transport Processes"]}]}, {"name": "Science", "subtopics": [{"name": "Chemistry", "specialties": ["Physical Chemistry"]}, {"name": "Physics", "specialties": ["High Energy Physics", "Nuclear Physics"]}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Exam Solutions", "Exams", "Lecture Notes", "Problem Sets"], "description": "This course describes the processes by which mass, momentum, and energy are transported in plasmas, with special reference to magnetic confinement fusion applications. The Fokker-Planck collision operator and its limiting forms, as well as collisional relaxation and equilibrium, are considered in detail. Special applications include a Lorentz gas, Brownian motion, alpha particles, and runaway electrons. The Braginskii formulation of classical collisional transport in general geometry based on the Fokker-Planck equation is presented. Neoclassical transport in tokamaks, which is sensitive to the details of the magnetic geometry, is considered in the high (Pfirsch-Schluter), low (banana) and intermediate (plateau) regimes of collisionality.", "course_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/22-616-plasma-transpo